  ✓ CONFIRMED: Caller-supplied proxy administrator permits unauthorized upg
    Justification: Upon reviewing AccountFactory.create and the proxy 
administrator logic in BeaconProxy and BaseProxy, I confirmed that the factory 
accepts an arbitrary caller-supplied administrator without authorization or 
ownership validation. AccountFactory.create(accountManager) is external and 
permissionless, and it directly constructs BeaconProxy(beacon, accountManager). 
BeaconProxy passes that value to BaseProxy._setAdmin, which only rejects 
address(0); it does not require the administrator to be the account owner, 
factory, governance, or another trusted address. The resulting administrator can
invoke changeBeacon() through the adminOnly modifier and replace the account’s 
beacon with an attacker-controlled beacon whose implementation contains 
arbitrary logic. Because the proxy delegates all account calls and may hold 
assets, this can enable asset theft if an integration creates or funds an 
account while assigning an attacker-controlled administrator. Beacon ownership 
does not mitigate this issue because the proxy administrator can change the 
beacon itself. The supplied code does not demonstrate a victim-facing 
integration flow, so exploitation against a funded third-party account depends 
on external usage, but the access-control flaw is directly present.
  ? UNCERTAIN: ERC4626 recursive pricing lacks cycle and depth protection
    Note: Upon reviewing ERC4626Oracle.getPrice, OracleFacade.getPrice and 
setOracle, and RiskEngine valuation functions, I confirmed that recursive 
pricing has no explicit maximum depth, visited-token tracking, or cycle 
validation. ERC4626Oracle.getPrice obtains the vault asset and calls 
oracleFacade.getPrice(asset), allowing a configured cycle such as vault A -> 
vault B -> vault A to recurse until the transaction runs out of gas and reverts.
RiskEngine._valueInWei invokes this path during balance, borrow, withdrawal, and
health checks, so affected operations could become unavailable. However, the 
demonstrated configuration entry point, OracleFacade.setOracle, is protected by 
adminOnly, and the supplied deployment does not deploy or register an 
ERC4626Oracle at all. Consequently, the source establishes a latent 
denial-of-service condition under malicious or erroneous privileged 
configuration, but it does not establish that an unprivileged attacker can cause
such registration. Determining whether this is a practically exploitable 
vulnerability requires the omitted ownership model, deployment configuration, 
and any permissionless oracle or vault-registration paths.
