  ✓ CONFIRMED: Aave V3 supply and withdraw recipients are not constrained t
    Justification: Upon reviewing AaveV3Controller.canCall in 
controller/src/aave/AaveV3Controller.sol, I confirmed that recipient binding is 
absent for both supported Aave operations. For SUPPLY, the controller decodes 
only the first argument, asset, from data[4:] and checks the corresponding 
aToken allowance; it never decodes or validates the third argument, onBehalfOf. 
For WITHDRAW, it likewise decodes only asset and does not inspect the third 
argument, to. ControllerFacade.canCall merely forwards calldata to the 
configured controller and adds no independent validation. Account.exec in 
protocol/src/core/Account.sol authorizes the accountManager but then forwards 
arbitrary calldata directly using target.call, without constraining protocol 
recipient parameters. Consequently, an otherwise authorized execution through 
this path can supply assets from the Account while crediting aToken ownership to
a different address, or withdraw Account-owned aTokens while directing 
underlying assets to an attacker-controlled recipient. The shown code contains 
no guard requiring onBehalfOf or to to equal the Account, so the hypothesis is 
exploitable assuming the normal controller-approved execution path invokes 
Account.exec.
  ✗ REJECTED: Aave V3 controller may permit calls to non-canonical pool ta
    Reason: Upon reviewing AaveV3Controller.canCall and 
