  ✗ REJECTED: ERC4626 calls are not bound to an approved vault or underlyi
    Reason: Upon reviewing the alleged missing ERC4626 target authorization in 
ControllerFacade.canCall and ERC4626Controller.canCall, I determined this is a 
false positive for the stated attack path. ControllerFacade does not route 
solely by selector: it first indexes controllerFor, which is populated only 
through the admin-only updateController function. Consequently, an arbitrary 
unapproved vault supplied by a caller has no controller and cannot pass 
validation through this facade. ERC4626Controller additionally calls 
IERC4626(target).asset() and derives the reported token flows from the selected 
target, so the code does not blindly assume an unrelated asset. Although there 
is no separate ERC4626 registry or explicit comparison against an expected 
underlying token, that omission does not permit a caller to introduce an 
unapproved target; configuring a malicious or mismatched vault would require the
trusted administrator to register it. Account.exec is also restricted by 
accountManagerOnly, providing an independent execution boundary. The provided 
code therefore shows administrative target allowlisting rather than 
selector-only authorization, although stronger per-vault asset metadata could 
improve defense in depth.
