  ✓ CONFIRMED: Only the input token is allowlisted, permitting unsupported 
    Justification: Upon reviewing UniV3Controller functions parseExactInput, 
parseExactOutput, and their single-swap and multicall variants, I confirmed 
incomplete token-path validation. For exact-output multihop swaps, Uniswap 
encodes the path in reverse order, and parseExactOutput validates only 
params.path.toAddress(params.path.length - ADDR_SIZE), the input token, while 
params.path.toAddress(0), the output token, is neither allowlisted nor otherwise
checked. Consequently, an allowlisted input can be swapped into an unsupported 
output. Conversely, parseExactInput validates only the final path address, the 
output token, leaving the input endpoint unchecked. No parser iterates through 
the path to validate intermediate hop tokens. The same endpoint-only behavior 
exists in the single-swap functions, and the multicall parsers preserve this 
gap. ControllerFacade merely delegates to the controller and adds no validation,
while the supplied RiskEngine and OracleFacade do not reject unsupported assets 
before valuation; OracleFacade only fails when no oracle exists. Thus, a 
properly formed route using an unsupported output or intermediate token can pass
controller validation, subject to available liquidity and downstream execution 
assumptions.
